// Regression test: ValueRangePass must reject an array access indexed by a
// LOCAL VARIABLE whose value is provably a constant out-of-bounds index --
// not just a literal index (see the pre-existing fail_array_out_of_bounds.tr,
// which only covers `arr[3]` written directly as a literal).
//
// Root cause: ValueRangePass::runOnCFG() resolved variables via
// GlobalSymbolTable->LookupSymbol(name) -- a lookup against the SymbolTable's
// *current* scope stack. This pass runs inside FuncDefExprAST::codegen()
// AFTER Body->codegen() has already fully executed, and BlockExprAST::codegen()
// unconditionally EnterScope()s/ExitScope()s around its statements -- including
// the function's own top-level body block. By the time ValueRangePass ran,
// that scope (and every local declared in it, e.g. `i` below) had already been
// popped, so LookupSymbol("i") always returned nullptr and constant tracking
// was silently a no-op for every local in the function: `mut i: int = 5;
// arr[i]` compiled with zero diagnostics and only failed at RUNTIME via the
// separate, existing bounds-check codegen path. Fixed by keying the pass's
// constant map on VariableExprAST/VarDeclExprAST's ResolvedSymbol (a
// VariableSymbol* set once by the Resolver and owned by the AST node itself,
// independent of the SymbolTable's scope-stack timing) -- the same fix
// already applied to DefiniteAssignmentPass, see Analysis.cpp.

fn main(): int {
    mut arr: int[3] = [10, 20, 30];
    mut i: int = 5;
    io::printline(arr[i]);
    return 0;
}
